# Mark / Highlight

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The `mark` element highlights a short piece of text that matters in the context a person is reading, such as the part that changed or the search word in a result. It is not for emphasis, and not for decoration.

This page owns the `mark` element. Home Assistant has no highlight component of its own. A dashboard author can use `mark` in a [Markdown card](markdown-card.html).

This page covers:

- Mark / Highlight
  - `<mark>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A highlight is one short run of text inside a sentence, with a sentence nearby that says why it is highlighted.

1. The marked text, usually a number, a time or a word.
2. The words that explain it, such as “The highlighted time is the part that changed.”

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-mark"><code>&lt;mark&gt;</code></h3>

The element has no attributes of its own. Browsers draw it with a yellow background and black text, and it is not interactive and not a link.

**In a Markdown card.** `mark` is kept, but every attribute is removed, so you cannot add a class, a colour, an id or an `aria` attribute. Home Assistant adds no style for it, so the browser’s default applies in light and dark mode alike. If you need a different highlight, write the meaning in words and use bold text.

**What the Civica theme sets.** Nothing. The theme has no variable for a highlight, so it stays as the browser draws it.

**Support.** Browsers treat `mark` as a plain run of text. Screen readers may not announce it, and some announce it as “highlight” or “mark” depending on settings. Do not rely on the highlight being announced.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It marks the part that matters without adding a new box or change of layout.
- For many readers the highlight is only a change of colour, so it cannot carry the meaning on its own.
- Its colour cannot be changed in a Markdown card, and the yellow stays in dark mode.
- A custom highlight colour needs its contrast checked against the text and the card. Do not assume it passes.
- A highlight grows with the text, so it still works when people enlarge text.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Highlight one thing in a block, and never a whole paragraph. Say why in words next to it, for example “the part that changed”. Do not use it for emphasis (use bold), for code (use code formatting) or to show a status. Never highlight text only to show it is new or wrong.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the meaning is in the text, not in the highlight.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: do not use the highlight as the only signal.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum) and 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: measure any colour pair you set yourself.
- 1.4.12 Text Spacing: the highlighted box must grow with the text.

